#usda 1.0
(
    doc = """Mesh-import fixture (task 1.4): two texCoord2f[] primvars and no
    'st', the first unusable and the second fine, so the scan has to discard
    what it learned from a candidate it ended up not using.

    DOCTEST ONLY -- deliberately has no check_godot.ps1 entry. What this file
    asserts is the ABSENCE of a warning on a successful import, and the golden
    checker only matches lines that must be PRESENT. A regression here adds a
    warning line and changes no geometry, so every needle it could carry would
    still match. Only CHECK(uvs_warn_flags == 0) catches it.

    TOPOLOGY -- the quad_mesh.usda quad
      points            4 entries
      faceVertexCounts  [4]
      faceVertexIndices [0, 1, 2, 3]

    THE TWO CANDIDATES
      primvars:BadUV   interpolation = "perCorner", which is not a USD
                       interpolation, so it resolves to no mode and fails.
      primvars:GoodUV  vertex, 4 values, perfectly usable.

      Neither is named 'st', so the named path never runs and the scan is the
      only way either is reached. BadUV's values are nothing like GoodUV's, so
      picking the wrong one is obvious in the UV assertion.

    EXPECTED
      - 4 vertices, 6 indices, [0, 1, 2, 0, 2, 3]
      - UVs after the V-flip, from GoodUV: (0,1) (0,0) (1,0) (1,1)
      - AABB spans (-1, 0, -1) to (1, 0, 1)
      - uvs_warn_flags == 0. BadUV's 'invalid interpolation mode' is raised
        while scanning and then cleared, because by the time GoodUV succeeds
        it describes a source the importer did not use.

    DETECTOR: this is the only test of the branch that clears accumulated
    scan warnings on success when no primary UV set exists. An implementation
    that clears only when 'st' was present -- which is what the first draft of
    the scan did -- imports these UVs correctly and still reports '[UVS]
    invalid interpolation mode', blaming a primvar it discarded.

    DEPENDS ON ENUMERATION ORDER, which USD does not document.
    UsdGeomPrimvarsAPI::GetPrimvarsWithValues() -- the list the scan walks --
    was observed to return primvars ordered by name on OpenUSD v26.05, which
    is why BadUV must sort before GoodUV. If GoodUV were reached first the
    scan would succeed immediately, no warning would ever be raised, and this
    file would pass while testing nothing.

    That failure is silent, so the doctest REQUIREs the order before asserting
    anything else. A pin bump that changes it fails there, loudly, rather than
    quietly hollowing this fixture out. Renaming either primvar carries the
    same hazard: the names are chosen to sort, not to describe."""
    defaultPrim = "Root"
    metersPerUnit = 1
    upAxis = "Y"
)

def Xform "Root"
{
    def Mesh "UvFallbackAfterBadCandidateQuad"
    {
        float3[] extent = [(-1, 0, -1), (1, 0, 1)]
        int[] faceVertexCounts = [4]
        int[] faceVertexIndices = [0, 1, 2, 3]
        point3f[] points = [(-1, 0, -1), (-1, 0, 1), (1, 0, 1), (1, 0, -1)]
        texCoord2f[] primvars:BadUV = [(0.1, 0.1), (0.1, 0.9), (0.9, 0.9), (0.9, 0.1)] (
            interpolation = "perCorner"
        )
        texCoord2f[] primvars:GoodUV = [(0, 0), (0, 1), (1, 1), (1, 0)] (
            interpolation = "vertex"
        )
    }
}
